            <ArticleTitle>Obesity and breast cancer: a clinical biochemistry perspective.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>189-97</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.clinbiochem.2011.11.016</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the laboratory diagnosis aspects of obesity-related health problems with special reference to postmenopausal breast cancer.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We conducted a systemic search of the literature primarily from the PubMed to obtain the relevant data.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Obesity is associated with the dysregulations of a number of body components such as blood constituents, extracellular matrix, and hormones/growth factors axes, which could be utilized for early diagnosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Obesity-related disorders including breast cancer have emerged as major health problems in almost all the nations. There is a need to elucidate different biochemical markers that are being used in the clinics or have the potential for such use. A precise understanding of the complex pathologies related with obesity is useful in prevention, early diagnosis and overall clinical management.</AbstractText>
